QB.add(73,[{"n":1,"q":"A 3-month-old infant presents with jaundice. Laboratory tests reveal elevated bilirubin levels, with a higher proportion of unconjugated bilirubin. The infant’s mother mentions that the jaundice has been present since birth and that the infant has not shown any signs of hemolysis. What is the most likely underlying cause of this infant's condition related to bilirubin metabolism?","o":[{"t":"Impaired uptake of bilirubin by hepatocytes"},{"t":"Defective conjugation of bilirubin due to UGT1A1 mutation"},{"t":"Reduced biliary excretion of bilirubin due to MRP2 mutation"},{"t":"Increased production of bilirubin due to hemolysis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Defective conjugation of bilirubin due to UGT1A1 mutation"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The infant’s jaundice is primarily due to elevated unconjugated bilirubin levels , indicating a problem with bilirubin processing. The mutation in the UGT1A1 gene leads to Crigler Najjar syndrome which disrupts the conjugation process, leading to an accumulation of unconjugated bilirubin in the liver."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bilirubin Metabolism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilirubin is the final product of heme degradation, with 70–90% originating from senescent red blood cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is transported to the liver bound to albumin due to its water insolubility."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatocellular Uptake Bilirubin uptake by hepatocytes occurs through carrier-mediated transport, although the specific transporter is not yet identified."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilirubin uptake by hepatocytes occurs through carrier-mediated transport, although the specific transporter is not yet identified."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intracellular Binding Inside hepatocytes, bilirubin binds to glutathione-S-transferases (previously called ligandins) to remain in solution."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inside hepatocytes, bilirubin binds to glutathione-S-transferases (previously called ligandins) to remain in solution."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conjugation Bilirubin is conjugated with glucuronic acid by UDP-glucuronosyltransferase ( UGT1A1 ), forming mono- and di-glucuronides. Conjugation disrupts hydrogen bonds, increasing bilirubin’s solubility and allowing excretion into bile. The UGT1 gene family, specifically UGT1A1 , is responsible for conjugation; mutations in these genes can affect isoform functions differently depending on the exon affected."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilirubin is conjugated with glucuronic acid by UDP-glucuronosyltransferase ( UGT1A1 ), forming mono- and di-glucuronides."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conjugation disrupts hydrogen bonds, increasing bilirubin’s solubility and allowing excretion into bile."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The UGT1 gene family, specifically UGT1A1 , is responsible for conjugation; mutations in these genes can affect isoform functions differently depending on the exon affected."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biliary Excretion Conjugated bilirubin (mono- and di-glucuronides) is excreted into bile via the ATP-dependent transporter MRP2 (ABCC2); mutations in MRP2 lead to Dubin-Johnson syndrome . Part of the conjugated bilirubin may enter portal circulation and undergo reuptake into hepatocytes by OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, which is defective in Rotor syndrome (SLCO1B1 and SLCO1B3 gene mutations)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conjugated bilirubin (mono- and di-glucuronides) is excreted into bile via the ATP-dependent transporter MRP2 (ABCC2); mutations in MRP2 lead to Dubin-Johnson syndrome ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Part of the conjugated bilirubin may enter portal circulation and undergo reuptake into hepatocytes by OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, which is defective in Rotor syndrome (SLCO1B1 and SLCO1B3 gene mutations)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilirubin uptake by hepatocytes occurs through carrier-mediated transport, although the specific transporter is not yet identified."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inside hepatocytes, bilirubin binds to glutathione-S-transferases (previously called ligandins) to remain in solution."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilirubin is conjugated with glucuronic acid by UDP-glucuronosyltransferase ( UGT1A1 ), forming mono- and di-glucuronides."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conjugation disrupts hydrogen bonds, increasing bilirubin’s solubility and allowing excretion into bile."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The UGT1 gene family, specifically UGT1A1 , is responsible for conjugation; mutations in these genes can affect isoform functions differently depending on the exon affected."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conjugated bilirubin (mono- and di-glucuronides) is excreted into bile via the ATP-dependent transporter MRP2 (ABCC2); mutations in MRP2 lead to Dubin-Johnson syndrome ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Part of the conjugated bilirubin may enter portal circulation and undergo reuptake into hepatocytes by OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, which is defective in Rotor syndrome (SLCO1B1 and SLCO1B3 gene mutations)"},{"k":"img","v":9610},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Impaired uptake of bilirubin by hepatocytes (Option A) : This would lead to increased unconjugated bilirubin levels, not elevated conjugated bilirubin, as seen in this infant. Thus, uptake is not the primary issue."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reduced biliary excretion of bilirubin due to MRP2 mutation (Option C) : While an MRP2 mutation could lead to elevated conjugated bilirubin, it’s less likely here since the infant shows no signs of cholestasis, making defective conjugation a more probable cause. Mutations in the MRP2 (ABCC2) gene disrupt bilirubin glucuronide transport across the canalicular membrane, causing bilirubin accumulation in hepatocytes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased production of bilirubin due to hemolysis (Option D) : Increased hemolysis would raise unconjugated bilirubin levels, but the absence of hemolysis signs in this infant makes this option inconsistent with the findings."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 17-year-old male presents to the clinic for a routine check-up. His laboratory tests reveal serum bilirubin levels of 45 mmol/L (2.6 mg/dL) with normal liver function tests. He reports occasional mild jaundice, particularly after periods of stress and fasting. There is no history of hemolysis or liver disease. The patient has been asymptomatic otherwise. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient based on the provided information?","o":[{"t":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type I"},{"t":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type II"},{"t":"Dubin-Johnson Syndrome"},{"t":"Gilbert Syndrome"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Gilbert Syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s mildly elevated unconjugated bilirubin levels, normal liver function tests , and intermittent jaundice during stress and fasting incidentally diagnosed in adolescence are all characteristic of Gilbert Syndrome ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Gilbert Syndro me"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"About Gilbert Syndrome (GS) is a common, benign condition"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"with mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and normal liver function tests , affecting up to 8% of people, more common in males."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiolo Caused by reduced UGT1A1 enzyme activity (10–35% gy of normal), often due to a TA insertion variant in the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"UGT1A1 promoter (A[TA]7TAA). Inheritance is typically autosomal recessive with the promoter variant; some cases show dominant inheritance with other mutations."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Featur Serum bilirubin <51 mmol/L (<3 mg/dL), fluctuating with es stress, fasting, alcohol, or illness; jaundice may occur"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"during elevations. Most patients are asymptomatic, though jaundice can occur during episodes of elevated bilirubin levels. Often diagnosed incidentally in adolescence or adulthood."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations Serum bilirubin levels are intermittently high, with"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Normal liver function tests and hepatic histology . UGT1A1 gene testing may reveal the A[TA]7TAA promoter variant."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Usually no treatment is needed. Lifestyle adjustments,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"such as avoiding alcohol and maintaining regular calorie intake, may help manage bilirubin levels. Phenobarbital can normalize bilirubin levels if required. Caution with drugs metabolized by UGT1A1 (e.g., irinotecan, HIV protease inhibitors) due to risk of toxicity."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type I (Option A) is characterized by severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (typically >20 mg/dL) due to the complete absence of UGT1A1 activity. Patients often exhibit significant jaundice from infancy and are at risk for kernicterus. The bilirubin levels in the scenario are too low for this diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type II (Option B) occurs due to reduced UGT1A1 activity (10-20% of normal) leading to unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia; it still presents with higher bilirubin levels than in Gilbert Syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dubin-Johnson Syndrome (Option C) is characterized by conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to defective biliary excretion of conjugated bilirubin due to multidrug resistance protein (MRP 2)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"defect leading to increased direct bilirubin in the serum. Liver function tests show abnormalresults, which are not consistent with the normal findings in this case."}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 2-week-old infant is brought to the emergency department due to jaundice. The mother reports that the infant’s skin has become increasingly yellow since birth. Laboratory tests reveal a serum bilirubin level of 450 mmol/L (26 mg/dL), and the infant appears lethargic. There are no signs of hemolysis, and a physical examination shows no other abnormalities. What is the most likely diagnosis for this infant based on the provided information?","o":[{"t":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type I"},{"t":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type II"},{"t":"Dubin-Johnson Syndrome"},{"t":"Rotor Syndrome"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type I"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The infant’s extremely high unconjugated bilirubin level ( 450 mmol/L) is characteristic of Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type I , which presents with severe, lifelong unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, typically starting in the neonatal period."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kernicterus risk is high due to the bilirubin level, which aligns with CN-I features. Crigler-Najjar Type II would present with lower bilirubin levels and a reduced risk of kernicterus."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hyperbilirubinemia syndromes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type I (CN-I) Gene: UGT1A1"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathology: Complete absence of bilirubin UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UDPGT) activity; No bilirubin conjugation occurs, leading to severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Severe jaundice from birth extreme hyperbilirubinemia (>342 mmol/L or >20 mg/dL) high risk of kernicterus , with neurological damage. Often fatal without intervention."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations Increased unconjugated bilirubin + conjugated bilirubin absent No signs of excessive hemolysis No bilirubin UDPGT activity– no response to phenobarbital No bilirubin glucuronides in bile (bile is pale or colorless)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Requires lifelong phototherapy; Liver transplantation is often definitive in preventing neurological damage."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type II (CN-II) Gene: UGT1A1 Pathology: Marked reduction (£10% of normal) in bilirubin UDPGT activity partial conjugation of bilirubin occurs"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Moderate jaundice with serum bilirubin levels of 103–428 mmol/L (6–25 mg/dL) kernicterus is possible under stress conditions (illness or surgery) Usually presents in infancy or childhood"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations Increased unconjugated bilirubin + conjugated bilirubin present Reduced bilirubin UDPGT activity; Unconjugated bilirubin levels decrease significantly in response to phenobarbital; Bile contains bilirubin monoglucuronide"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Phenobarbital therapy to induce residual UDPGT activity"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dubin-Johnson Syndrome (Option C) Gene: ABCC2 gene"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathology: Mutations in the MRP2 (ABCC2) gene disrupt bilirubin glucuronide transport across the canalicular membrane, causing bilirubin accumulation in hepatocytes."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Asymptomatic or with mild symptoms Mild to moderate jaundice that can darken with age Hepatocytes accumulate dark pigment but liver function is normal."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations Increased conjugated bilirubin Darkly pigmented liver on biopsy Positive urinary coproporphyrin I levels."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Usually, no treatment is required; manage jaundice as needed"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Rotor syndrome (Option D) Gene: SLCO1B1 and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"SLCO1B3 gene"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathology: Affect the reuptake system (OATP1B1 and OATP1B3) for bilirubin glucuronides, causing some bilirubin to re-enter the circulation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Mild to moderate jaundice; No dark liver pigmentation as in Dubin-Johnson"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations Direct hyperbilirubinemia I ncreased urinary coproporphyrin I and III levels Liver is not pigmented on biopsy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Usually no treatment required; manage jaundice as needed"}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 30-year-old woman presents to the clinic with intermittent jaundice that worsens during her menstrual cycle and when taking oral contraceptives. Laboratory tests reveal elevated conjugated bilirubin levels, but her liver function tests are otherwise normal. A liver ultrasound shows no significant abnormalities, and she has no history of significant liver disease. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient based on the provided information?","o":[{"t":"Dubin-Johnson Syndrome"},{"t":"Rotor Syndrome"},{"t":"Budd-Chiari Syndrome"},{"t":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type II"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Dubin-Johnson Syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s symptoms of intermittent jaundice, elevated conjugated bilirubin levels, and normal liver function tests are characteristic of Dubin-Johnson Syndrome. The exacerbation of symptoms during hormonal changes (menstrual cycle and oral contraceptives) aligns with known triggers for this benign condition."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rotor Syndrome (Option B) is similar to Dubin-Johnson but usually presents with less severe conjugated hyperbilirubinemia and does not have the characteristic black liver. The lab findings and clinical features in this case are more consistent with Dubin-Johnson."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Budd-Chiari Syndrome (Option C) involves hepatic venous outflow obstruction, leading to abdominal pain, ascites, and liver dysfunction. The patient's normal liver function tests and lack of significant clinical symptoms do not support this diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type II (Option D) is associated with moderate unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, not conjugated. The patient’s elevated conjugated bilirubin levels and the presence of triggers (hormonal changes) do not fit with Crigler-Najjar Type II."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 28-year-old man presents to the clinic with intermittent jaundice and dark urine. His laboratory tests show elevated conjugated bilirubin levels and urine tests reveal bilirubinuria. An ultrasound of the liver is unremarkable, and a cholecystography shows a normal gallbladder. The patient has no significant past medical history and is otherwise asymptomatic. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient based on the provided information?","o":[{"t":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type I"},{"t":"Gilbert Syndrome"},{"t":"Rotor Syndrome"},{"t":"Dubin-Johnson Syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Rotor Syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s elevated conjugated bilirubin levels, presence of bilirubinuria, and normal appearance of the liver with visualization of the gallbladder are characteristics of Rotor Syndrome. This condition is similar to Dubin-Johnson but lacks the liver pigmentation associated with Dubin-Johnson Syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Crigler-Najjar Syndrome Type I (Option A) involves severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to absent UGT1A1 activity, which would not present with elevated conjugated bilirubin levels as seen in this patient."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gilbert Syndrome (Option B) is characterized by mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and is typically asymptomatic. The presence of elevated conjugated bilirubin and bilirubinuria in this case makes Gilbert Syndrome an unlikely diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dubin-Johnson Syndrome (Option D) presents with elevated conjugated bilirubin and bilirubinuria but is associated with a characteristic black liver due to pigment accumulation, which is not noted in this patient. The gallbladder is also visualized, unlike in Dubin-Johnson."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 35-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with abdominal pain, severe ascites, and jaundice that developed over the past month. On examination, she has hepatomegaly and splenomegaly. Laboratory tests show elevated liver enzymes and bilirubin levels. A Doppler ultrasound of the abdomen reveals occlusion of multiple hepatic veins. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient based on the provided information?","o":[{"t":"Dubin-Johnson Syndrome"},{"t":"Budd-Chiari Syndrome"},{"t":"Gilbert Syndrome"},{"t":"Rotor Syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Budd-Chiari Syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s symptoms of abdominal pain, ascites, jaundice, and hepatomegaly, combined with the ultrasound findings of hepatic vein occlusion, are characteristic of Budd-Chiari Syndrome, which involves hepatic venous outflow obstruction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dubin-Johnson Syndrome (Option A) is characterised by elevated conjugated bilirubin due to defective biliary excretion and typically presents with mild jaundice. The severe abdominal symptoms and hepatic vein occlusion in this case do not fit this diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gilbert Syndrome (Option C) is associated with mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and is usually asymptomatic. The acute symptoms of abdominal pain, ascites, and jaundice indicate a much more serious condition, not consistent with Gilbert Syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rotor Syndrome (Option D) presents with elevated conjugated bilirubin and bilirubinuria but does not involve significant abdominal pain or ascites. The findings of hepatic vein occlusion and severe liver symptoms are not characteristic of Rotor Syndrome."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 32-year-old woman presents with 2 months of progressive abdominal pain and distension. On examination, she has spider angiomas, hepatomegaly, and moderate ascites. Laboratory studies show haemoglobin 18.2 g/dL, WBC 13,000/mL, platelets 650,000/mL, and normal liver enzymes. Doppler ultrasound reveals hepatic vein thrombosis. Which of the following is the most common cause of the suspected condition?","o":[{"t":"Myeloproliferative disorders"},{"t":"Vitamin B12 deficiency"},{"t":"Inflammatory bowel disease"},{"t":"Chronic hepatitis C"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Myeloproliferative disorders"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The patient's clinical findings are suggestive of Budd-Chiari Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Classic triad present: Abdominal pain Hepatomegaly Ascites"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abdominal pain"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascites"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Confirmatory imaging: Hepatic vein thrombosis on Doppler"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Compatible timeline: Subacute presentation (2 months)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abdominal pain"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ascites"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The most common causes of Budd-Chiari Syndrome by frequency:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myeloproliferative disorders: ~50% of cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pregnancy/OCPs: ~20% of cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Malignancy: ~10% of cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Idiopathic: ~20% of cases"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Budd-Chiari Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Category Specific Causes Frequency Clinical Features"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myeloproliferative Polycythemia vera ~50% Elevated blood counts Disorders Essential thrombocythemi Splenomegaly"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"a Thrombotic events Primary myelofibrosis Pruritus Occult MPD"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypercoagulable States Factor V Leiden Variable Recurrent thrombosis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Antiphospholipid Young age syndrome Family history Protein C/S deficiency Multiple sites Antithrombin deficiency PNH"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hormonal Oral contraceptives ~20% Female gender"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pregnancy Reproductive age Hormone replacement Recent medication"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"changes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Malignancy Hepatocellular carcinoma ~10% Mass lesions"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Renal cell carcinoma Weight loss Adrenal tumors Constitutional symptoms Lymphoma Paraneoplastic features"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vitamin B12 deficiency (Option B): This condition, while sometimes associated with elevated haemoglobin and platelets, is not a typical cause of hepatic vein thrombosis or Budd-Chiari syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Inflammatory bowel disease (Option C): Although inflammatory bowel disease affects the gastrointestinal tract, it is not directly linked to hepatic vein thrombosis or liver-specific complications like Budd-Chiari syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic hepatitis C (Option D): Chronic hepatitis C can lead to liver disease but is not a recognised cause of hepatic vein thrombosis or Budd-Chiari syndrome."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 35-year-old woman presents with 3 weeks of progressive abdominal pain and ascites. Laboratory studies show elevated LFTs and a prolonged PT. Doppler ultrasound reveals hepatic vein thrombosis. After initial diagnosis, which of the following represents the most appropriate sequential management approach?","o":[{"t":"Proceed directly to liver transplantation"},{"t":"Initiate anticoagulation, followed by assessment for predisposing causes and TIPS if needed"},{"t":"Start diuretics alone and monitor symptoms"},{"t":"Perform surgical shunting as primary therapy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Initiate anticoagulation, followed by assessment for predisposing causes and TIPS if needed"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: Diagnosis of Budd-Chiari syndrome is supported by:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Classic clinical triad: Severe abdominal pain Rapidly developing ascites Hepatomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe abdominal pain"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rapidly developing ascites"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Confirmatory imaging: hepatic vein thrombosis on Doppler"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute presentation (<2 weeks)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Evidence of liver dysfunction (elevated INR and LFTs)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Severe abdominal pain"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rapidly developing ascites"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatomegaly"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management follows a stepwise approach:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Budd-Chiari Syndrome- Management"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Category Components Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Features Classical triad: Acute: rapid onset (<2 weeks), no"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Abdominal pain collaterals Ascites Chronic: gradual onset, established Hepatomegaly collaterals May have jaundice Spider angiomas splenomegaly"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Initial Diagnosis Imaging studies Doppler US: first line (85-90%"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Laboratory tests sensitivity) Diagnostic paracentesis CT/MRI if needed"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"LFTs, coagulation profile Ascitic fluid: high protein, SAAG >1.1"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Etiologic Workup MPD evaluation JAK2 mutation testing"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Hypercoagulable testing Thrombophilia workup Other investigations Pregnancy test"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Screen for malignancy"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Initial Management Anticoagulation LMWH fi Warfarin"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Supportive care Manage ascites Treatment of cause Treat underlying condition"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Monitor response"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Step-up Therapy Interventional procedures Thrombolysis if acute"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Advanced options TIPS for failed medical therapy"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Angioplasty/stenting Surgical shunts"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Liver Transplantation Indications: 3-year survival ~85%"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Fulminant failure Need lifelong anticoagulation Failed other therapy Regular monitoring Advanced cirrhosis Consider early in fulminant cases"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Proceeding directly to liver transplantation (Option A) is typically reserved for cases with liver failure unresponsive to other treatments. Initial management generally includes anticoagulation and evaluation of underlying causes before considering transplantation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Starting diuretics alone and monitoring symptoms (Option C) may help manage ascites but do not address the underlying thrombosis or prevent further clotting. Anticoagulation is necessary to reduce clot progression in hepatic vein thrombosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Performing surgical shunting as primary therapy (Option D) is not a first-line treatment; it’s typically considered if anticoagulation and other interventions like TIPS fail to control symptoms or improve liver function."}]},{"n":9,"q":"Which of the following statements is true regarding how the Bromsulphthalein test helps distinguish between Rotor syndrome and Dubin-Johnson syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Dubin johnson syndrome - prolonged retention of BSP in the bloodstream Rotor syndrome - BSP is rapidly cleared."},{"t":"Dubin Johnson syndrome - delayed excretion with prolonged retention of BSP occurs, Rotor syndrome - early excretion with no delayed retention."},{"t":"Both Rotor syndrome and DJS show prolonged retention of BSP, making the test ineffective in differentiation."},{"t":"Dubin Johnson syndrome - BSP is taken up slowly by the liver but is excreted rapidly, Rotor syndrome - BSP is taken up rapidly by the liver but is excreted slowly"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Dubin johnson syndrome - delayed excretion with prolonged retention of BSP occurs; Rotor syndrome - early excretion with no delayed retention"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: BSP test shows prolonged retention of the dye in Dubin-Johnson syndrome due to impaired excretion, while in Rotor syndrome , the dye is excreted promptly without delayed retention. This difference in BSP handling helps in distinguishing between the two syndromes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bromsulphthalein (BSP) test"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The BSP test involves injecting bromsulphthalein dye into the bloodstream and measuring its retention and excretion by the liver."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normally , the liver should process and excrete BSP efficiently, with minimal retention i n the blood after a set period."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Delayed or prolonged retention of BSP suggests an issue with the liver's excretory function, which can vary between DJS and Rotor syndrome. DJS - Prolonged retention of the dye indicating impaired excretion similar to the buildup of bilirubin Rotor syndrome - no prolonged retention of dye; the liver is still able to process and excrete the dye without significant delay."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DJS - Prolonged retention of the dye indicating impaired excretion similar to the buildup of bilirubin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rotor syndrome - no prolonged retention of dye; the liver is still able to process and excrete the dye without significant delay."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DJS - Prolonged retention of the dye indicating impaired excretion similar to the buildup of bilirubin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rotor syndrome - no prolonged retention of dye; the liver is still able to process and excrete the dye without significant delay."},{"k":"img","v":9627}]}]);
QB.imgs({"9610":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","9627":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});